deep.reconstruction:
Setting (continuing from p.91 = ms p.45). A 'courbe spéciale' X is a nodal curve, possibly punctured, whose combinatorial type is recorded by a dual graph G. G consists of:
- a vertex set S (the irreducible components);
- an edge set A (the nodes), together with the half-edges Ã (preimages of the nodes in the normalization Y) and the origin map σ_G: Ã → S;
- the marked points I, with a map I → S.
The completed curve is X̂ = Y/σ_Ã and X = (Y∖I)/σ_Ã. A 'G-épinglage' is a rigidification identifying the components, nodes and marked points of X with the corresponding pieces of G, up to specialization data.

On the previous page, a subset A' ⊂ A is chosen and X̂ is partially normalized at the nodes in A∖A'. This gives X̂' = Y/σ_{Ã'}. X̂' carries a canonical set of marked points I ⊔ J̃, where J̃ = Ã∖Ã' is the set of branches over the unglued nodes, so Card J̃ = 2·Card(A∖A').

This page generalizes. Instead of keeping only a subset I' ⊂ I as punctures, one keeps an arbitrary subset I' ⊂ I ⊔ J̃ as the new marked points. Given (G, A' ⊂ A(G), I' ⊂ I ⊔ J̃), every G-pinned special curve yields a G'-pinned special curve. Here G' has:
- vertex set S;
- edge set A';
- legs I', mapped to S by formula (126): I' → S induced by I → S on I and by the origin map Ã(G) → S on J̃;
- the specialization data inherited from G.

In modern language this is the functoriality of strata of M̄_{g,n} (or of the moduli of nodal curves with dual graph G) under two operations:
1. 'ungluing' a set of edges, which is the inverse of the Knudsen clutching/gluing morphisms;
2. forgetting some legs, including newly created half-edge legs.
It is the operation that turns the boundary stratum M_G ≅ ∏_v M_{g_v,n_v} into its factors. It is a step toward the category of graphs and the 'structure at infinity' (the neighbourhood of the boundary) that the notes study. The next page generalizes further to subgraphs with a vertex subset S' ⊂ S, and singles out the local star at a vertex α, where I' = I_α ⊔ Ã_α.
